Z||000000||JohnnyK||09-30-2001||04:15 AM||||I was warming up my car (95 accord) getting ready for me to go home (IE, I let it run for 15 minutes while I was sitting outside) and then I sat in the car and realized my feet were slippery, so I looked out, and there was a long trail of oil, so I looked under, and checked my dipstick, and there was NO oil in the block (The engine could have been brand new for all the oil I found in there). It's 4am so I can't really do a good diagnostics, but it's at my friends house now. I'll have to check it out tomorrow. This is not good at all.||||reg||1
Z||000001||hugh||09-30-2001||07:54 AM||||To lose that much oil that fast,it must be a high pressure leak.Maybe your oil filter is leaking.||||reg||5
Z||000002||SteveJ||09-30-2001||08:28 AM||||This worked out well for you in that you didn't drive without oil. Because you didn't say any dash lights came on I'd say you did no damage to the engine.<BR>But as an aside. No manufacturer recommends a warm-up period, much less 15 minutes. The days when cars stalled if driven cold are over. But I am glad it worked out for you.<p>[This message has been edited by SteveJ (edited 09-30-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||stimpy||09-30-2001||10:18 AM||||A good reason to ALWAYS use Mobil1 in your car. You should be alright once you fix the leak. Hondas are pretty tough.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Formula||09-30-2001||10:49 AM||||why would it matter if he used mobil1 or pennzoil? <P>does mobil1 stop leaks or something??<P>I'm in the dark on this one.||||reg||1
Z||000005||FieroFanaticus2||09-30-2001||01:13 PM||||I use pennzoil in all of my cars.  I've never had any problems.  I don't think oil makes too much of a difference when it comes to leaks, except for the thicker the oil the slower the leak.  Just don't use the bottom of the barrel brands.||||reg||1
Z||000006||JohnnyK||09-30-2001||01:23 PM||||I get the feeling the dash lights don't work, because there was absolutely no oil in there, and to come out that fast, there must have been no pressure left in there. I wasn't really giving it a "warm-up" period.. It was just really cold out and I was at a party, so I figured I'd warm up the inside of the car before I left. Anyways, I gotta go tow it home now. I'll let you guys know.||||reg||1
Z||000007||JohnnyK||09-30-2001||04:28 PM||||Seems to be coming out around the timing chain/belt area. Wouldn't a main seal have a slower leak? Not "5L is gone in 1 hour"?||||reg||1
Z||000008||JohnnyK||09-30-2001||05:43 PM||||Well, I think its coming from there. Seems there "used' to be a rubber seal around the plastic casing for the timing and the front of the engine. Now about 3 inches is ripped and hanging down. So if that part is full of oil, I guess there is no belt in there eh? What about cars that have a timing belt.. Still oil in there?||||reg||1
Z||000009||stimpy||09-30-2001||07:27 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Formula:<BR><B>why would it matter if he used mobil1 or pennzoil? <P>does mobil1 stop leaks or something??<P>I'm in the dark on this one.</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah, I guess that was a bit abmiguous. The advantage that I think would be with synthetic is that even if the oilpan drops out, there is still going to be some residual oil on the moving parts. Since synthetic takes longer to lose viscosity, I'd feel safer. Plus, if that were to happen with a hot engine, synthetic isn't prone to burst into flame a la 84 2M4's.||||reg||1
